ight loss", "Hypercalcemia", "Elevated alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia and Elevated Alkaline Phosphatase", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3482142857142857, "posterior": 0.3542394969304364, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Secondary hyperparathyroidism is associated with high urine calcium and bone-related symptoms, which are absent in this case. This weakens the metabolic bone d…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Medications/Toxins with Hypercalcemia", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.039, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Medication-induced hypercalcemia typically does not cause significant ALP elevation or weight loss. These findings are more indicative of an underlying maligna…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Metabolic Bone Disease with Secondary Hyperparathyroidism", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08928571428571429, "posterior": 0.11500038895162973, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Secondary hyperparathyroidism is associated with high urine calcium and bone-related symptoms, which are absent in this case. This weakens the metabolic bone d…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Sarcoidosis with Hypercalcemia and Elevated Alkaline Phosphatase", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.164, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Viral Illness with Constitutional Symptoms and Transient Alkaline Phosphatase Elevation", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.328, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low 24-hour urine sodium (5 mmol) and subacute presentation with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, neurological changes) argue for malignancy-related hypercalcemia rather than primary hy…", "summary": "The subacute presentation, constitutional symptoms, and low urine sodium are more consistent with malignancy than primary hyperparathyroidism, which typically presents with normocalciuria or hypercalciuria."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's history of antacid use for GERD and low 24-hour urine sodium (5 mmol) support a diagnosis of milk-alkali syndrome?", "summary": "Milk-alkali syndrome is characterized by high urine calcium and sodium, which are absent here. The low urine sodium and lack of hypercalciuria make this diagnosis unlikely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's antacid use (GERD treatment) and absence of high urine calcium (5 mmol/24h) create a safer alternative explanation than malignancy?", "summary": "Antacid use alone cannot explain the combination of hypercalcemia, elevated ALP, and constitutional symptoms. The absence of high urine calcium further weakens alternative diagnoses."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) and recent weight loss (5 pounds) inconsistent with a diagnosis of medication-induced hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Medication-induced hypercalcemia typically does not cause significant ALP elevation or weight loss. These findings are more indicative of an underlying malignancy."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the low 24-hour urine sodium (5 mmol) and absence of bone pain/fractures inconsistent with secondary hyperparathyroidism?", "summary": "Secondary hyperparathyroidism is associated with high urine calcium and bone-related symptoms, which are absent in this case. This weakens the metabolic bone disease hypothesis."}], "differential_history": [], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": ""}, "turn_budget_used": 0, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.348, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.0, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Hyperparathyroidism'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia and Elevated Alkaline Phosphatase", "posterior": 0.354, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 4, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B5", "B4", "B3"]}}, "parent_branch": {"id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia and Elevated Alkaline Phosphatase", "level": 1, "posterior": 0.3542394969304364, "danger": 0.8, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation, constitutional symptoms, and low urine sodium are more consistent with malignancy than primary hyperparathyroidism, which typically presents with normocalciuria or hypercalciuria.", "Antacid use alone cannot explain the combination of hypercalcemia, elevated ALP, and constitutional symptoms. The absence of high urine calcium further weakens alternative diagnoses.", "Medication-induced hypercalcemia typically does not cause significant ALP elevation or weight loss. These findings are more indicative of an underlying malignancy.", "Secondary hyperparathyroidism is associated with high urine calcium and bone-related symptoms, which are absent in this case. This weakens the metabolic bone disease hypothesis."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Any history of known cancer or suspicious symptoms (e.g., bone pain, palpable lymphadenopathy)", "Duration and progression of constitutional symptoms"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Imaging studies (chest/abdomen/pelvis CT, bone scan)", "Parathyroid hormone (PTH) and PTH-related protein (PTHrp) levels", "Serum protein electrophoresis"]}, "target_level": 2}
